(D7 — the VARIABLE-form sub-case is RESOLVED (vox #44), re-recorded
 2026-08-22 against vox 0.4.9. Originally: a list or map interpolated
 into a format string via the VARIABLE form (`"{flat}"`) rendered
 correctly in PRINT position and rendered a raw heap address everywhere
 else. old manual lines 3082-3085 (now re-pinned in the FMT ledger's own
 range) says all sinks "share one name resolver" and render "identically
 whether the result is printed, written to a file, or built into a
 buffer"; that promise now holds for this sub-case.
 NOTE: the EXPRESSION form (`"{element 2 of nested}"`) is a DIFFERENT,
 still-open sub-case — see collections-b.md's own D7 and vox candidate
 #68. This probe only ever tested the variable form.
 The address used to CHANGE BETWEEN RUNS, so this probe prints stable
 verdicts instead of the raw addresses (that is still the right way to
 probe it, even now that both verdicts read "ok").
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox D7.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   print position: [1, 2, 3]
   text initializer: ok
   buffer sink: ok
)
a list called flat is [1, 2, 3].
print "print position: {flat}".
a text called captured is "{flat}".
If captured is equal to "[1, 2, 3]" then, print "text initializer: ok". Otherwise, print "text initializer: WRONG".
a buffer called sink is 64 bytes in size.
copy "{flat}" to sink.
a text called drained is "{sink}".
If drained is equal to "[1, 2, 3]" then, print "buffer sink: ok". Otherwise, print "buffer sink: WRONG".
